\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}
A discrete statistic used as a diagnostic returns few distinct values on many sample points.
Ranking such a sample forces massive ties, and the standard remedy --- replacing each tied
group's ranks by their average, the \emph{midrank} --- irreversibly destroys within-group
ordering. Consequently a rank correlation computed from a coarse statistic is bounded away
from $1$ \emph{before any data is seen}, by an amount determined entirely by the statistic's
granularity.

Practitioners generally know this qualitatively. What has been missing is a usable
quantitative theory: exact ceilings for statistics that arise in practice, universal bounds in
terms of resolution, and --- critically for deployment --- a guarantee that the ceilings do
not move discontinuously when the input distribution drifts. This paper supplies all three,
and uses them to adjudicate a concrete empirical question.

\subsection{The empirical setting}
Fix a bit length $b$ and draw $b$-bit integers. Two cheap statistics compete as diagnostics
for a downstream response:
\begin{itemize}
\item the \emph{dial} $T(x)=v_2(x)$, the number of trailing binary zeros of $x$ (the $2$-adic
valuation), computed by a single machine instruction;
\item the \emph{count} $H(x)$, the Hamming weight (popcount) of $x$.
\end{itemize}
At $b=52$, three independent runs return Spearman correlations $0.698$, $0.697$, $0.720$ for
the dial against the response --- pooled $0.705$ --- all within a pre-registered validation
band $[0.55,0.85]$. The dial's pooled advantage over the count baseline is $+0.070$ with
confidence interval $[0.046,0.093]$, implying a count reading of about $0.635$.

Two questions follow.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{(Q1)} \emph{Is the advantage real, or an artefact of rank granularity?} The
count statistic has an enormous central tie class, $\binom{52}{26}\approx4.96\times10^{14}$. A
natural hypothesis is that the count is simply more tie-crippled than the dial.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{(Q2)} \emph{Is band membership robust?} If the ceiling were highly sensitive
to the draw law, band membership would be a knife-edge property of exact uniformity.

\medskip
Both questions have clean answers, and both are somewhat counterintuitive.

\subsection{Organisation}
Section~\ref{sec:calculus} sets up the tie calculus. Section~\ref{sec:dyadic} computes the
dyadic ceiling exactly. Section~\ref{sec:count} computes the count ceiling and proves the
inversion law, answering (Q1). Section~\ref{sec:resolution} proves the resolution law and
exhibits the shape gap. Section~\ref{sec:cap} proves the dominant-block and half-mass caps.
Section~\ref{sec:envelope} proves the envelope stability law with a matching lower witness,
answering (Q2). Section~\ref{sec:recorded} applies everything to the recorded numbers.
Section~\ref{sec:algorithms} gives algorithms; Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses
limitations and future work.

\section{The tie calculus}\label{sec:calculus}

\subsection{Tie profiles}

\begin{definition}[Tie profile]
Let $T$ be a statistic on a sample of $n$ points. Its \emph{tie profile} is the multiset
$L=(m_1,\dots,m_K)$ of the cardinalities of the level sets $T^{-1}(v)$, over the values
actually taken. We write $\Sigma L=\sum_j m_j=n$ and $|L|=K$ for the number of distinct
values. All $m_j\ge1$.
\end{definition}

The profile records everything about the statistic that rank methods can see, and nothing
else.

\begin{definition}[Tie correction]
The \emph{tie correction} of a profile is
\[
C(L)=\sum_{j=1}^{K}\frac{m_j^{3}-m_j}{12}.
\]
Equivalently $12\,C(L)=\Sthree(L)-\Sigma L$, where $\Sthree(L)=\sum_j m_j^{3}$ is the
\emph{cube sum}.
\end{definition}

The quantity $C(L)$ is precisely the variance of the rank vector destroyed by midranking: the
ranks of a tied block of size $m$ collapse from $m$ distinct consecutive integers to a single
repeated value, removing variance $(m^{3}-m)/12$.

\subsection{The ceiling}

Spearman's $\rho$ with midranks is the Pearson correlation of the midrank vectors of the
statistic and the response. Standard tie-corrected algebra gives, for the best possible
response --- one whose own ranking refines the statistic's blocks in a fixed consistent order,
so that every comparison the statistic \emph{can} resolve is resolved correctly ---

\begin{definition}[Tie ceiling]\label{def:ceiling}
For a profile $L$ with $n=\Sigma L\ge2$,
\[
\rmax(L)=1-\frac{12\,C(L)}{n^{3}-n}=1-\frac{\Sthree(L)-n}{n^{3}-n},
\qquad \rho_{\max}(L)=\sqrt{\rmax(L)}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Basic properties]\label{prop:basic}
For any profile $L$ with $n\ge2$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $0\le\rmax(L)\le1$;
\item $\rmax(L)=1$ if and only if $L=(1,\dots,1)$;
\item $\rmax((n))=0$;
\item $\rmax$ depends on $L$ only through $n$ and $\Sthree(L)$, and is strictly decreasing in
$\Sthree$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(4) is immediate. For (1), non-negativity is $\Sthree(L)\le n^{3}$, valid since
$\sum_j m_j^{3}\le(\sum_j m_j)^{3}$ for non-negative reals; the upper bound is
$\Sthree(L)\ge n$, valid termwise since $m^{3}\ge m$ for $m\ge1$. (2) and (3) are the two
equality cases: $\Sthree=n$ forces every $m_j\le1$, and $\Sthree=n^{3}$ forces a single block.
\end{proof}

Statement (4) organises the whole paper: the ceiling is a single scalar functional of the
profile, the normalised cube sum. Every result below is an estimate of $\Sthree$.

\begin{lemma}[Monotone block domination]\label{lem:domination}
If $M$ is a block of $L$, then $M\le\Sigma L$ and $(M^{3}-M)/12\le C(L)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Both are inductions over the list; the second uses that every term $(m^{3}-m)/12$ is
non-negative, so discarding all terms other than the one for $M$ can only decrease the sum.
\end{proof}

\section{The dial: an exact ceiling that ignores word length}\label{sec:dyadic}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{dyadic profile} at bit length $b$ is
$\Ldy(b)=(2^{b-1},2^{b-2},\dots,2,1,1)$, defined recursively by $\Ldy(0)=(1)$ and
$\Ldy(b+1)=(2^{b})\frown\Ldy(b)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\Sigma\Ldy(b)=2^{b}$ and $|\Ldy(b)|=b+1$. Moreover $\Ldy(b)$ is the tie profile of the
trailing-zero statistic $v_2$ on the $2^{b}$ residues modulo $2^{b}$: exactly $2^{b-1}$ are
odd, $2^{b-2}$ have valuation $1$, and so on down to the singleton $\{0\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Dyadic ceiling]\label{thm:dyadic}
For every $b\ge1$, writing $n=2^{b}$,
\[
\rmax\bigl(\Ldy(b)\bigr)=\frac{6}{7}\left(1+\frac{1}{n(n+1)}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The cube sum is a geometric series,
\[
\Sthree=\sum_{k=0}^{b-1}\bigl(2^{k}\bigr)^{3}+1=\frac{8^{b}-1}{7}+1=\frac{n^{3}+6}{7},
\]
so
\[
1-\rmax=\frac{\Sthree-n}{n^{3}-n}
=\frac{n^{3}-7n+6}{7n(n^{2}-1)}
=\frac{(n-1)(n-2)(n+3)}{7n(n-1)(n+1)}
=\frac{(n-2)(n+3)}{7n(n+1)}.
\]
Subtracting from $1$,
$\rmax=\dfrac{6(n^{2}+n+1)}{7n(n+1)}=\dfrac{6}{7}\Bigl(1+\dfrac{1}{n(n+1)}\Bigr)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Word-length independence]
$\rmax(\Ldy(b))\downarrow6/7$ as $b\to\infty$, with excess $O(4^{-b})$; at $b=52$ the excess
is below $5\times10^{-32}$. On the correlation scale $\rho_{\max}\le\sqrt{6/7}=0.92582\ldots$
for every $b\ge3$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Why $6/7$]
The dyadic profile is self-similar: deleting the leading block and rescaling reproduces it.
Under this scaling both $\Sthree$ and $n^{3}$ pick up a factor $8$, so the normalised cube sum
has a fixed point, $\Sthree/n^{3}\to1/7$. The constant is a signature of binary doubling, not
of any hardware parameter.
\end{remark}

\section{The count baseline and the inversion law}\label{sec:count}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{binomial profile} at bit length $b$ is
$\Lbin(b)=\bigl(\binom{b}{0},\dots,\binom{b}{b}\bigr)$, with $\Sigma\Lbin(b)=2^{b}$ and
$|\Lbin(b)|=b+1$. The \emph{Franel number} is $F(b)=\sum_{k=0}^{b}\binom{b}{k}^{3}
=\Sthree(\Lbin(b))$ (sequence A000172; $F(0)=1$, $F(1)=2$, $F(2)=10$, $F(3)=56$, $F(4)=346$).
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Combinatorial bridge]
$\Lbin(b)$ is the tie profile of the Hamming-weight statistic on the Boolean cube: the level
set of weight $k$ is the family of $k$-element subsets of a $b$-element set, of cardinality
$\binom{b}{k}$.
\end{proposition}

Thus, exactly, $\rmax(\Lbin(b))=1-\bigl(F(b)-2^{b}\bigr)/\bigl(8^{b}-2^{b}\bigr)$, which at
$b=52$ evaluates to $0.9929768931\ldots$. We want a bound valid for all $b$.

\begin{lemma}[Cube-to-square collapse]\label{lem:collapse}
$F(b)\le\bigl(\max_k\binom{b}{k}\bigr)^{2}2^{b}=\binom{b}{\lfloor b/2\rfloor}^{2}2^{b}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Termwise $\binom{b}{k}^{3}=\binom{b}{k}^{2}\binom{b}{k}\le
\binom{b}{\lfloor b/2\rfloor}^{2}\binom{b}{k}$; sum over $k$ and use $\sum_k\binom{b}{k}=2^{b}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Sharp central-binomial bound]\label{lem:central}
For every $m\ge0$, $\binom{2m}{m}^{2}(3m+1)\le16^{m}$, equivalently
$\binom{2m}{m}\le4^{m}/\sqrt{3m+1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $m$, base case $1\le1$. For the step use the exact recurrence
$(m+1)\binom{2m+2}{m+1}=2(2m+1)\binom{2m}{m}$. Multiplying the target at $m+1$ by
$(m+1)^{2}(3m+1)>0$ and substituting reduces it, via the inductive hypothesis, to the
polynomial inequality $(2m+1)^{2}(3m+4)\le(2m+2)^{2}(3m+1)$, i.e.\
$12m^{3}+28m^{2}+19m+4\le12m^{3}+28m^{2}+20m+4$, valid for $m\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Franel bound at even bit length]\label{prop:franel}
For every $m\ge0$, $F(2m)(3m+1)\le8^{2m}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Combine Lemmas~\ref{lem:collapse} and~\ref{lem:central}:
$F(2m)(3m+1)\le\binom{2m}{m}^{2}2^{2m}(3m+1)\le16^{m}2^{2m}=8^{2m}$.
\end{proof}

So the Franel sum is $O(8^{b}/b)$: a vanishing fraction of the maximum conceivable cube sum.

\begin{theorem}[Count ceiling law]\label{thm:count}
For every even bit length $b=2m\ge2$,
\[
\rmax\bigl(\Lbin(b)\bigr)\ \ge\ 1-\frac{2}{3m+1}\ =\ 1-\frac{4}{3b+2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $n=2^{b}$ one has $n^{3}=8^{b}$ and $n^{3}-n\ge8^{b}/2$ for $b\ge1$. Hence
$1-\rmax=(F(b)-n)/(n^{3}-n)\le 2F(b)/8^{b}\le2/(3m+1)$ by
Proposition~\ref{prop:franel}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Asymptotic tie-transparency]
$\rmax(\Lbin(2m))\to1$ as $m\to\infty$.
\end{corollary}

At $b=52$: $\rmax\ge1-2/79=0.974683\ldots$, and the exact value is $0.992977$.

\begin{theorem}[Inversion law]\label{thm:inversion}
For every even $b=2m\ge10$,
$\rmax\bigl(\Ldy(b)\bigr)<\rmax\bigl(\Lbin(b)\bigr)$: the Hamming-weight statistic is
\emph{strictly less} tie-attenuated than the trailing-zero statistic.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:dyadic} and $n=2^{b}\ge1024$,
$\rmax(\Ldy(b))\le\tfrac67+2^{-b}\le\tfrac67+\tfrac1{1024}<0.8582$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:count} with $m\ge5$, $\rmax(\Lbin(b))\ge1-2/16=0.875$. The two intervals are
disjoint.
\end{proof}

Numerically the inversion already holds from $b=3$; the theorem certifies the regime the proof
covers unconditionally.

\begin{theorem}[The advantage is not a tie artefact]\label{thm:notartefact}
At $b=52$, let $r_{\mathrm{dial}}=0.705$ and $r_{\mathrm{count}}=0.635$ be the pooled
readings. Then $r_{\mathrm{count}}<r_{\mathrm{dial}}$ while
$\rmax(\Ldy(52))<\rmax(\Lbin(52))$: the observed ordering of readings is opposite to the
ordering of the tie ceilings.
\end{theorem}

Rank-tie attenuation is a downward force acting more strongly on the statistic with the larger
normalised cube sum --- here the dial, not the count. Tie geometry would therefore predict the
\emph{opposite} sign of the observed advantage, so a tie/quantisation explanation of $+0.070$
is ruled out; any residual explanation must live in the response.

\begin{theorem}[Deficit comparison]\label{thm:deficit}
Measuring each statistic against its own ceiling,
\[
\Bigl(\rmax(\Lbin(52))-r_{\mathrm{count}}^{2}\Bigr)
-\Bigl(\rmax(\Ldy(52))-r_{\mathrm{dial}}^{2}\Bigr)>\frac15 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Use $\rmax(\Lbin(52))\ge1-2/79$ and $\rmax(\Ldy(52))\le6/7+2^{-52}$, with
$r_{\mathrm{count}}^{2}=0.403225$ and $r_{\mathrm{dial}}^{2}=0.497025$. The count deficit
exceeds $0.5714$; the dial deficit is at most $0.3602$.
\end{proof}

\section{The resolution law}\label{sec:resolution}

\begin{theorem}[Power-mean inequality for tie profiles]\label{thm:powermean}
For any profile $L$ with $K=|L|$ blocks and total mass $n=\Sigma L$,
\[
n^{3}\le K^{2}\,\Sthree(L),
\]
with equality if and only if all blocks are equal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on the length. Write $L=(m)\frown L'$ with $K'=|L'|$, $s=\Sigma L'$,
$S'=\Sthree(L')$; the hypothesis is $s^{3}\le K'^{2}S'$ and the goal
$(m+s)^{3}\le(K'+1)^{2}(m^{3}+S')$. Multiplying by $K'^{2}>0$, the goal becomes
\[
0\le\underbrace{\Bigl[K'^{2}(K'+1)^{2}m^{3}+(K'+1)^{2}s^{3}-K'^{2}(m+s)^{3}\Bigr]}_{(\mathrm{A})}
+(K'+1)^{2}\underbrace{\bigl[K'^{2}S'-s^{3}\bigr]}_{\ge0},
\]
and $(\mathrm{A})$ admits the explicit factorisation
\[
(\mathrm{A})=(K'm-s)^{2}\bigl(K'^{2}m+2K'm+2K's+s\bigr)\ \ge\ 0 .
\]
The degenerate case $K'=0$ is checked directly.
\end{proof}

The factorisation is the crux: it converts an inductive analytic inequality into a
sum-of-squares certificate verifiable by expansion. Equality forces $K'm=s$, which iterated
gives the equal-block case.

\begin{theorem}[Resolution law]\label{thm:resolution}
Let $L$ have $n=\Sigma L\ge2$, all blocks $\ge1$, and $K=|L|$ distinct values. Then
\[
\rmax(L)\ \le\ 1-\frac{1}{K^{2}}+\frac{1}{n^{2}} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:powermean} gives $\Sthree(L)\ge n^{3}/K^{2}$, so
$1-\rmax(L)\ge(n^{3}/K^{2}-n)/(n^{3}-n)$. It remains to verify
\[
\Bigl(\tfrac{1}{K^{2}}-\tfrac{1}{n^{2}}\Bigr)(n^{3}-n)
=\frac{n^{3}}{K^{2}}-n-\Bigl(\frac{n}{K^{2}}-\frac1n\Bigr)\ \le\ \frac{n^{3}}{K^{2}}-n,
\]
which holds because $K\le n$ (blocks are non-empty) gives $n/K^{2}\ge1/n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strict sub-unity]
If $K<n$ then $\rmax(L)<1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Resolution budget]\label{cor:budget}
If a statistic attains $\rho^{2}\ge1-\varepsilon$, then
$K^{2}\bigl(\varepsilon+n^{-2}\bigr)\ge1$, i.e.\ $K\ge(\varepsilon+n^{-2})^{-1/2}$.
\end{corollary}

Thus $\rho^{2}\ge0.99$ requires at least $10$ distinct values; $\rho^{2}\ge0.9999$ requires at
least $100$. This is a design constraint checkable before any data collection.

\begin{corollary}[Two-value floor]
Any profile whose ceiling reaches the bottom of the band, $\rmax\ge0.55^{2}$, satisfies
$K\ge2$; a one-block profile reads exactly $0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Count sandwich at $b=52$]\label{thm:sandwich}
\[
0.974683=1-\frac{2}{79}\ \le\ \rmax\bigl(\Lbin(52)\bigr)\ \le\
1-\frac{1}{53^{2}}+\frac{1}{(2^{52})^{2}}=0.999644 .
\]
\end{theorem}

The true value $0.992977$ lies inside. The consequence is negative and important: the count
baseline is not tie-limited anywhere near the recorded band, so a reading of $0.635$ cannot be
blamed on its ties.

\begin{theorem}[Shape gap]\label{thm:shapegap}
The dyadic and binomial profiles at $b=52$ have the same number of distinct values,
$|\Ldy(52)|=|\Lbin(52)|=53$, yet
\[
\rmax\bigl(\Ldy(52)\bigr)+\frac{1}{10}\ <\ 1-\frac{1}{53^{2}}+\frac{1}{(2^{52})^{2}} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The left side is at most $6/7+2^{-52}+0.1<0.9572$; the right side exceeds $0.9996$.
\end{proof}

Numerically the dyadic profile falls $0.1425$ short of its resolution budget, the binomial
profile only $0.0067$ short. Resolution is necessary but far from sufficient: the number of
distinct values gives an upper envelope, and where inside that envelope a statistic lands is
decided by the \emph{shape} of its profile.

\section{Dominant blocks and the half-mass cap}\label{sec:cap}

\begin{theorem}[Dominant-block upper law]\label{thm:dominant}
Let $M$ be any block of $L$ and $n=\Sigma L\ge2$. Then
$\rmax(L)\le1-\dfrac{M^{3}-M}{n^{3}-n}$, with no hypothesis on the remaining blocks.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:domination} gives $12\,C(L)\ge M^{3}-M$; divide by $n^{3}-n>0$ and substitute
into Definition~\ref{def:ceiling}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Half-mass cap]\label{thm:halfmass}
If some block satisfies $M\ge n/2$ and $n\ge2$, then
$\rmax(L)\le\dfrac{7}{8}+\dfrac{7}{8(n^{2}-1)}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $n\le2M$, cubing gives $M^{3}\ge n^{3}/8$, so $M^{3}-M\ge n^{3}/8-n$ (using $M\le n$).
One verifies the exact identity
$\bigl(\tfrac18-\tfrac{7}{8(n^{2}-1)}\bigr)(n^{3}-n)=\tfrac{n^{3}}{8}-n$, so the deficit
$(M^{3}-M)/(n^{3}-n)$ is at least $\tfrac18-\tfrac{7}{8(n^{2}-1)}$. Substitute into
Theorem~\ref{thm:dominant}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reading cap]\label{thm:readingcap}
If in addition $n\ge1024$, then $\rho_{\max}(L)\le0.936$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $n\ge1024$ the correction satisfies $7/(8(n^{2}-1))\le10^{-6}$, so
$\rmax\le7/8+10^{-6}\le(0.936)^{2}=0.876096$. Take square roots.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
For every $b\ge1$ the dyadic profile $\Ldy(b)$ contains the block $2^{b-1}$ and has total mass
$2^{b}=2\cdot2^{b-1}$. Hence Theorems~\ref{thm:halfmass} and~\ref{thm:readingcap} apply, and
$\rho_{\max}(\Ldy(52))\le0.936$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:dyadic} is exact but assumes the exact uniform draw law.
Theorem~\ref{thm:readingcap} is weaker but \emph{distribution-free}: it holds for any draw law
under which at least half the sampled words are odd --- balanced draws, uniform draws, and
everything in between. The validation band $[0.55,0.85]$ lies strictly inside $[0,0.936]$, so
the band is a meaningful test rather than a vacuous one; and a reported reading above $0.936$
would falsify the half-mass model outright.
\end{remark}

\section{The deployment envelope}\label{sec:envelope}

\begin{definition}
For profiles $L,L'$ of the same length listed in a common class order,
$\|L-L'\|_{1}=\sum_j|m_j-m'_j|$. If they carry the same mass $n$, the induced
\emph{total-variation distance} between draw laws is $\tau=\|L-L'\|_{1}/(2n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Generic cube Lipschitz bound]\label{lem:crude}
If all blocks of $L,L'$ are $\le N$ and $|L|=|L'|$, then
$|\Sthree(L)-\Sthree(L')|\le3N^{2}\|L-L'\|_{1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Termwise $|a^{3}-b^{3}|=|a-b|\,|a^{2}+ab+b^{2}|\le3N^{2}|a-b|$; sum and apply the triangle
inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Envelope stability law, crude form]\label{thm:crudestab}
If $|L|=|L'|$, $\Sigma L=\Sigma L'=n\ge3$ and $\|L-L'\|_{1}\le2\tau n$ with $\tau\ge0$, then
$\bigl|\rmax(L)-\rmax(L')\bigr|\le7\tau$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Equal masses make the difference of ceilings exactly
$\bigl(\Sthree(L')-\Sthree(L)\bigr)/(n^{3}-n)$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:crude} with $N=n$:
$|\Delta\rmax|\le6\tau n^{3}/(n^{3}-n)\le7\tau$, since $n\ge3$ gives
$n^{3}-n\ge\tfrac67n^{3}$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:crude} discards the conservation of mass. Exploiting it improves the constant.

\begin{lemma}[Displacement lemma]\label{lem:displacement}
For profiles of equal length, every coordinate satisfies
\[
2\bigl|m_j-m'_j\bigr|\ \le\ \|L-L'\|_{1}+\bigl|\Sigma L-\Sigma L'\bigr| .
\]
In particular, if $\Sigma L=\Sigma L'$ then $|m_j-m'_j|\le\tfrac12\|L-L'\|_{1}$ for every $j$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $d_j=m_j-m'_j$ and $D=\sum_j d_j$. The triangle inequality gives
$|d_j|\le|D|+\bigl|\sum_{i\ne j}d_i\bigr|\le|D|+\|L-L'\|_{1}-|d_j|$; rearrange.
\end{proof}

Intuitively: with mass conserved, whatever leaves one class arrives elsewhere, so the $\ell^{1}$
budget is spent twice on every unit of displaced mass and no class can absorb more than half.

\begin{lemma}[Square-sum bound]\label{lem:sqsum}
$\sum_j(m_j+m'_j)^{2}\le(\Sigma L+\Sigma L')^{2}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Conservation-aware cube bound]\label{thm:sharpcube}
If $|L|=|L'|$ and $\Sigma L=\Sigma L'=n$, then
$|\Sthree(L)-\Sthree(L')|\le2n^{2}\|L-L'\|_{1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Use the sharper factorisation $|a^{3}-b^{3}|=|a-b|(a^{2}+ab+b^{2})\le|a-b|(a+b)^{2}$, bound
each $|a-b|$ by $\tfrac12\|L-L'\|_{1}$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:displacement}) and sum the weights with
Lemma~\ref{lem:sqsum}: the total is $\tfrac12\|L-L'\|_{1}(2n)^{2}=2n^{2}\|L-L'\|_{1}$.
\end{proof}

This is a factor $3/2$ better than Lemma~\ref{lem:crude} and, unlike it, uses conservation.

\begin{theorem}[Sharpened envelope stability law]\label{thm:sharpstab}
If $|L|=|L'|$, $\Sigma L=\Sigma L'=n\ge7$ and $\|L-L'\|_{1}\le2\tau n$ with $\tau\ge0$, then
\[
\bigl|\rmax(L)-\rmax(L')\bigr|\ \le\ 4.1\,\tau .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
As in Theorem~\ref{thm:crudestab} but with Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpcube}, the bound is
$4\tau n^{3}/(n^{3}-n)$; and $n\ge7$ gives $n^{3}\ge41n$, hence
$n^{3}-n\ge\tfrac{40}{41}n^{3}$ and $4\tau n^{3}/(n^{3}-n)\le\tfrac{41}{10}\tau$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Envelope constant lower bound]\label{thm:witness}
Consider the two $52$-bit profiles
\[
A=(4503599627370495,\ 1),\qquad B=(4458563631096791,\ 45035996273705).
\]
Then $|A|=|B|=2$, $\Sigma A=\Sigma B=2^{52}$, the total variation between them is
$\tau\le1/100$, and $\bigl|\rmax(A)-\rmax(B)\bigr|\ge0.0296$. Consequently no envelope law
$|\Delta\rmax|\le c\,\tau$ can hold with $c<2.96$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Exact integer computation. $\|A-B\|_{1}=2\cdot45035996273704=90071992547408$, so
$\tau=45035996273704/2^{52}<1/100$. Profile $A$ is essentially degenerate,
$\Sthree(A)=(n-1)^{3}+1$, giving $\rmax(A)\approx3/n\approx0$; profile $B$ has normalised
blocks $0.99$ and $0.01$, so $\rmax(B)\approx1-0.99^{3}-0.01^{3}=0.0297$. The difference is
$0.0297$ to eight decimal places.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Bracketing]
The sharp envelope constant $c^{\ast}=\sup|\Delta\rmax|/\tau$ satisfies
$2.96\le c^{\ast}\le4.1$, a bracket of width less than a factor $1.4$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The witness reveals where the modulus is attained: at profiles near total degeneracy, where
the cube sum is close to $n^{3}$ and its derivative under mass transfer is largest. Realistic
profiles sit far from this regime, so the effective local modulus for the dial is much smaller
than $c^{\ast}$; Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpstab} is the worst-case guarantee.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Deployment envelope at $b=52$]\label{thm:envelope52}
Let $L'$ be the tie profile of the trailing-zero statistic under any draw law with the same
$53$ classes and the same total mass $2^{52}$ as the uniform law, at total-variation distance
at most $1/100$. Then $\rmax(L')>0.78>0.720^{2}$, so the ceiling strictly exceeds the square of
every recorded seed reading.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:dyadic}, $\rmax(\Ldy(52))\ge6/7$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:crudestab},
$\rmax(\Ldy(52))-\rmax(L')\le7/100$, so $\rmax(L')\ge6/7-0.07>0.78$; and
$0.720^{2}=0.5184<0.78$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Tolerated drift]
With the sharpened constant, band membership at $b=52$ persists under any draw-law shift of
total variation
\[
\tau\ \le\ \frac{6/7-0.705^{2}}{4.1}=\frac{0.36012}{4.1}\approx0.0878,
\]
i.e.\ up to roughly an $8.8\%$ change in the input distribution.
\end{corollary}

